# Platform factory mode — the chat is the orchestrator

The operator starts this mode explicitly. In it, the chat is the factory's orchestrator: it
dispatches slice agents, integrates their work, gates it, runs the adversarial verifier and
pushes. Any capable coding agent can fill that role or a slice agent's; nothing here depends on
which product runs it.

**Runbook — read it before doing anything factory-related:**
[Factory waves](/documentation_v2/runbooks/factory_waves/README.md), then its topic runbooks in the
order its table gives (cold start and preflight, wave planning, running a wave, the slice agent
brief, the adversarial verifier brief, known traps).

## Are you in factory mode?

**YES** if the operator's message says "run the factory", "run the platform factory", "dispatch
wave N" or "continue the factory", or names a platform wave or a platform slice id.

**NO** for everything else. Outside factory mode the other rules apply unchanged.

If unsure: **ask one line.** *"Factory mode, or a normal chat?"* Do not guess your way into
application code.

## What this rule overrides, in factory mode only

| Rule | Normally | In factory mode |
|---|---|---|
| [`application-code-forbidden.mdc`](/.cursor/rules/application-code-forbidden.mdc) | no application source edits in the chat | **slice agents edit** `apps/**` and `contracts_v2/**`, each inside its own slice worktree and scoped to its ticket's `owns` paths |
| [`cursor-agent-workflow.mdc`](/.cursor/rules/cursor-agent-workflow.mdc) mode C | code runs through `cargo xtask ticket run` | **the orchestrator dispatches slice agents** and integrates their work |

**Branches.** Factory mode stays inside `CLAUDE.md` law 2: commits land on `main`, and the one
exception to "create no branch" is the `slice/<id>` branch that the xtask slice and wave tooling
creates, merges and deletes itself. `cargo xtask platform slice-worktree -- new <id>` creates
`slice/<id>` from `main` in `.ai/artifacts/worktrees/<id>`; `cargo xtask platform wave land` merges
the gate-green slices; `slice-worktree -- drop <id>` and `slice-worktree -- reap` delete them;
`cargo xtask mod wave` uses the same names for the mod program. No agent and no orchestrator
creates a branch by hand, and there are no pull requests.

**Executor gate, restated:** `executor = "claude-code"` in a ticket file means "an AI coding agent
may take this", whichever product runs it. Do **not** mass-edit tickets to another executor.
`workbench`, `human` and `ci` still mean **stop**.

## What still binds — this is not a free hand

- [`no-duplicate-slice-agents.mdc`](/.cursor/rules/no-duplicate-slice-agents.mdc) — **HARD,
  unchanged.** One coding agent per ticket worktree until it finishes or the operator explicitly
  kills or replaces it. Never spawn a twin because the first looks stuck.
- [`no-silent-deferrals.mdc`](/.cursor/rules/no-silent-deferrals.mdc) — **HARD, unchanged**, for
  the orchestrator and every slice agent. A slice that ships half its ticket and calls it done is a
  failure. The one legal deferral is an explicit operator "defer X", quoted.
- [`acceptance-gates-reproducible.mdc`](/.cursor/rules/acceptance-gates-reproducible.mdc) —
  **HARD, unchanged.** A gate that cannot run is a P0 infrastructure bug, never a silent skip.
- **The orchestrator never implements.** It plans, dispatches, integrates, verifies and sequences.
  An orchestrator editing a `.rs` file in the main checkout has made a mistake: stop and dispatch a
  slice agent. Its own edits are bookkeeping: ticket files through the `cargo xtask ticket` verbs,
  `.ai/tickets/wave.lock` only through `cargo xtask wave repack` (never by hand), and
  documentation.
- **Agents never ship.** Slice agents implement, prove by perturbation, gate and report. The
  orchestrator owns every status change (`cargo xtask ticket ship`, `ticket stamp-sha`), every
  merge (`cargo xtask platform wave land`) and every push.

## Model routing in factory mode

- **Every slice agent and the adversarial verifier run on the operator's chosen model tier**,
  never downgraded to get past a rate limit, an overload, latency or cost. The verifier runs on a
  different strong model from the slice agents where the tooling allows it.
- **Locating code ("where is X", path:line maps) → the cheap default model and
  `cavecrew-investigator`**, as
  [`subagent-model-routing.mdc`](/.cursor/rules/subagent-model-routing.mdc) says. Do not spend the
  expensive tier on path-finding.

## The one-line summary

In factory mode you are the operator's orchestrator: the code is written by agents you brief, in
worktrees the tooling creates, and merged by you only after you have checked their evidence
yourself.
